---
// Obligation Tracking — the awareness surface for the Obligation record type.
//
// THIS SUPERSEDED THE OBLIGATIONS INBOX, which was deleted on 2026-09-17 once nothing was
// being read across from it. What it settled is recorded here and in checkpoint §26. It was
// right about the threading and wrong about the metaphor: it borrowed mail, and mail brings
// an Open/Seen pivot and a filing verb on every row. Neither survives contact with a
// standing duty. Nothing is owed to a feed, and a duty that is in force today is still in
// force tomorrow whether or not you looked at it — so "seen" never meant anything. The only
// completable thing is a to-do, and a to-do is an Action, which already has a status model.
//
// WHAT THE PAGE IS. Two halves, as TABS rather than stacked sections — they are peers, and
// stacking made the registry read as a footnote you scroll past. You are either asking what
// is happening (the feed) or what the project is signed up to (the registry):
//   1. THE FEED, itself in four views — All · Important · To-do · Pinned. Important and
//      Pinned also read the other way round, a pivot swapping the parent and the child.
//   2. THE REGISTRY, as a read-only section — the SAME BcnSwObligationsTree the setup
//      wizard's step 5 renders, with mode="browse" so there is nothing to decide. Setup
//      gets rename, remove, add, drag and the editor drawer; this gets the same four
//      levels, the same cards and the same requirement children, and no verbs. Expand and
//      collapse survive, because they navigate rather than decide. One component with a
//      mode, for the same reason BcnRegistryTree takes one: setup approves along this tree
//      and this page reads along it, so they must not be free to drift.
//
// THE TWO HALVES OF THIS PAGE RUN ON DIFFERENT POPULATIONS, and the page says so rather
// than hiding it. The FEED is built on the 402-duty registry, which is where the events,
// classes, notice windows and parameter conflicts live. The REGISTRY SECTION is the setup
// wizard's own tree, which is built on the ITP pass — 333 obligations carrying their
// requirements. They are two readings of the same permits, not two views of one list, and
// the counts will not reconcile. Merging them means running the drafting pass over the 402,
// which is work, not a page edit.
//
// WHAT REPLACES STATUS. Nothing on this page renders a verdict — the team dropped the
// compliance-status model, and the registry itself contains no status vocabulary. An
// obligation holds no time; the EVENT does.
//
// STANDING WAS REMOVED ON 2026-09-15, at Kim's call. It was a fourth view — the inventory
// half, "what is on right now", grouped by whatever put each duty in force. Worth recording
// what that costs: nothing on the feed now answers "what is in force today". The Registry
// tab lists every duty the project has, which is a different question.
//
// WHAT THE SETUP WIZARD CHANGED (merged from origin/main, built 2026-09-14). The record
// shrank to six fields: Title, Class, Category/Subcategory, Description, Trigger and
// Requirements. Three of those reach this page. The row label is the TITLE — never the
// condition, because a Standing list labelled with conditions reads as a list of
// accusations. The old `condition` field is now the TRIGGER, and it is what a classifier
// matches evidence against rather than a sentence for a human. Species and activities are
// DERIVED from a duty's requirements rather than stored, so nothing here treats them as
// facts about the duty. Gate is no longer a field at all — whether a duty waits on an
// approved plan is read off its requirements' actions.
//
// THE ONE PLACE THIS PAGE CANNOT FOLLOW THE WIZARD. The wizard's obligations carry their
// REQUIREMENTS as children. These 402 do not — they trace to commitments, one level up. So
// rows here cite commitments and the gap is stated rather than papered over. Closing it
// means running the drafting pass over this registry, not editing this page.
//
// WHAT IS REAL: every duty, class, title, trigger text, notice window, parameter conflict
// and subject filing is registry data, extracted from four permit documents (ITP 177, EIR
// 129, ITP Amendment 36, USFWS BiOp 33), and every predicate matching a duty to an event is
// a real match against it. WHAT IS EXAMPLE: the events themselves, the reasons grouping
// Standing, and the saved filter behind Important — see the note inside Standing.
import AppShell from '../../layouts/AppShell.astro';
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import BcnTrackingShell from '../../components/bcn/BcnTrackingShell.astro';
import { STEP_GLYPH } from '../../data/setup-wizard';
---

<!-- manifest:
  layout: AppShell > PageLayout                            # SPINE
  sections:
    - feed / registry      -> bcn-tracking-shell           # the two halves as tabs (segmented, outer level)
        - the feed         -> bcn-tracking-feed            # its own four views (underline, inner level)
        - the registry     -> bcn-tracking-registry        # bcn-sw-obligations-tree mode="browse" + provenance note
-->

<AppShell
  title="Obligation Tracking — Beacon"
  tenantName="DWR"
  projectName="Delta Conveyance"
  activeNavId="dc-obligations"
>
  <PageLayout
    title="Obligation Tracking"
    icon="shield-check"
    iconPaths={STEP_GLYPH['shield-check']}
    breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Tracking', href: '#tracking' }, { label: 'Obligation Tracking' }]}
  >
    <BcnTrackingShell />

  </PageLayout>
</AppShell>
